  "text": {
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LEORA_BELLFOUND": "Leora Bellfound",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LEORA_BELLFOUND_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Mist Bells[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Leora was recasting a cracked abbey bell when she realized that none of the valley hamlets could hear the fort's warning. She used the abbey's bronze allotment to build a chain of smaller towers, staffed in turn by shepherds and deserters. When an officer came to requisition the new bells, every hamlet rang its refusal.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEREN_HALFLIGHT": "Beren Halflight",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEREN_HALFLIGHT_PEDIA": "Captain of the Homeward Column[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Beren led a defeated column home through country its own quartermasters had stripped bare. He traded the horses first, then the officers' spurs, keeping a tally of every farmer who fed them. The farmers now come to him when conscription parties arrive, and the old column assembles without a drum.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HAMIRA_STONEFORD": "Hamira Stoneford",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HAMIRA_STONEFORD_PEDIA": "Reeve of the Third Ford[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]At flood season Hamira commandeered a noble's empty granary to keep the ford open. Her ferry carried every traveler regardless of rank, provided their fee paid for another stone in the approach. Now she can turn back a duke's whole levy by lifting the chain and pocketing the handle.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CEDRIC_STAVE": "Cedric Stave",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CEDRIC_STAVE_PEDIA": "Marshal of the Hallowed Mile[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Cedric's patrol brought back a holy banner from a road where three whole pilgrim companies had disappeared. In return he was given the width of a spear on either side of that road and the power to try anyone who set foot in it. He has spent the years since paying surveyors to discover how wide a spear can be.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DALIA_CHALKWALL": "Dalia Chalkwall",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DALIA_CHALKWALL_PEDIA": "The Barracks Schoolmaster[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dalia was put in charge of recruits who could recite whole prayers but could never check a pay docket. She chalked the articles of service on the wall, where the recruits began leaving questions under every line. When the wall was plastered over, her pupils walked out with the questions memorized and started schools of their own.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ZAHIR_WHITEFLINT": "Zahir Whiteflint",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ZAHIR_WHITEFLINT_PEDIA": "The Road Under the Road[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Zahir explored cool tunnels beneath abandoned caravan roads while serving as a water bearer. When a debt caravan camped above one of the entrances, he brought its prisoners out one family at a time. His guides now count a route as sacred if a person can escape along it without paying.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAFIYA_OF_THE_KILN": "Safiya of the Kiln",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SAFIYA_OF_THE_KILN_PEDIA": "The Glass Gardener[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Safiya grew up among glassblowers who tinted every window in the oasis except those of their own houses. She bought a heap of failed panes and laid them over a bed of roots, which survived the coldest desert night anyone remembered. People travel farther for a share of her kiln than they once traveled to pay court to the well lords.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KAMAL_RED_MERIDIAN": "Kamal Red Meridian",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KAMAL_RED_MERIDIAN_PEDIA": "Warden of the Midday Gate[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Kamal once shut his pass a minute late and lost a caravan to the raiders following it. He has fixed the gate hours to a bronze sight ever since, raising the bar for anyone whose shelter gives an enemy cover. The people who ask him to bend the rule usually end up living under the gate and working off their petition.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LUMA_DUNEBRIGHT": "Luma Dunebright",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LUMA_DUNEBRIGHT_PEDIA": "The Festival of Returning Feet[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]For years Luma rehearsed songs that her scattered kin could recognize without agreeing on all the words. She carried the best ones to the empty market at which her grandparents had met, and a few listeners put up tents. When they returned next year they brought roof beams, and Luma learned to sing loud enough to be heard over the work.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEDIM_SALTWIND": "Nedim Saltwind",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NEDIM_SALTWIND_PEDIA": "Master of the Buried Salt[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nedim was blinded in one eye after mistaking a salt lake's crust for firm ground. He sent apprentices back with long-handled rakes and paid them first from whatever they managed to bring out. His crews now auction their work from camp to camp, leaving richer towns to wonder why nobody has come to dig.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FARAH_QUIETWATER": "Farah Quietwater",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FARAH_QUIETWATER_PEDIA": "Custodian of the Shared Bank[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Farah kept a pair of flood markers on opposite banks because neither village would trust the other to read its own. One spring both markers vanished, and paddlers from both villages followed her to recover them. Their shared embankments outlasted the emergency, even after both abbots began arguing about who could claim the work as a miracle.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MATTEO_LINDEN": "Matteo Linden",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MATTEO_LINDEN_PEDIA": "The Lay Host[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Matteo's sister came home from a pilgrimage barefoot after a steward refused their family offerings. He bought a ruined bakehouse and left the door open to the first stranger who reached it. There are now lay houses in six provinces, each with a table long enough to seat both pilgrims and the people they are fleeing.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IMENA_WAKEFOLD": "Imena Wakefold",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IMENA_WAKEFOLD_PEDIA": "Warden of the Unmarked Sleep[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Imena cleared a ruined chapterhouse and found that the dirt beneath its floor whispered living people's names. Her order marked the site on no maps and used the stones to build an ordinary sheepfold. It now shelters families whose ancestors would be instruments in somebody else's hands if the fold's true boundary became known.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LEVAN_PLEDGE": "Levan Pledge",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LEVAN_PLEDGE_PEDIA": "The Collector of Sanctuary[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Levan started collecting vows of thanks while the refugees in his care still had no money. He kept each on a reed slip and redeemed small promises first, for a morning's labor or a winter blanket. The bundle has grown hefty enough that he can decide who marries, who travels, and which children will keep the sanctuary books.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ENNA_BRAMBLEHOOK": "Enna Bramblehook",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ENNA_BRAMBLEHOOK_PEDIA": "Gardener of the Siege Acre[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Enna watched the gardens inside a siege ring go to seed as most of the remaining land was requisitioned for troops. She grafted bitter roots onto fruiting plants, then sent the cuttings to households too poor to buy seed. When the walls reopened, local families kept exchanging plants through her long after the old ration masters had run out of work.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AURETH_FEATHER_ASH": "Aureth Feather-Ash",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AURETH_FEATHER_ASH_PEDIA": "Herald of the Borrowed Body[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Aureth's first body still carried a wooden badge carved by the soldier's daughter. He tracked down the family and found a line of others asking the host for possessions no officer had thought to collect. His companies now return one keepsake for every body they borrow and ask the survivors to name the next city they defend.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MERIEL_BRONZE_DAWN": "Meriel Bronze Dawn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MERIEL_BRONZE_DAWN_PEDIA": "Master of the Landing Steps[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Meriel watched the first winged regiment crush a water tower when its soldiers landed in an unfamiliar square. She devised stepped stone platforms that could take the weight and leave the wells accessible. The engineers who build them remain after the troops march, arguing that a garrison is no better than its stairs.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CARADRIEL_PALE_FIRE": "Caradriel Pale Fire",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CARADRIEL_PALE_FIRE_PEDIA": "The Relentless Dawn[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Caradriel's company took an unguarded gate and lost half its borrowed bodies to a demon sheltering in a grain store. She brought the grain scale back to camp and made it the emblem of a tribunal that could seize supplies on suspicion alone. Her towns now run on confiscated grain and pray each morning to remain above suspicion.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SORAD_VEILED_SEAL": "Sorad Veiled Seal",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SORAD_VEILED_SEAL_PEDIA": "The Annalist of Wings[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sorad watched two villages claim the same angel after each lost a son at a disputed crossing. He established a waiting house for such cases and began recording what the angels could recall before they heard anybody's testimony. The house answers to him today because too many officers want its ledgers to endorse a convenient ancestry.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AVONA_OF_TWO_WALLS": "Avona of Two Walls",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AVONA_OF_TWO_WALLS_PEDIA": "Bridge Between Companies[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Avona carried messages between the city guard and an angelic unit whose commanders refused to share a table. She had two small benches built into the chasm wall so they could confer while both sides watched. By the second siege the benches wore smooth, and the troops called for Avona when their commanders disagreed.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRAM_COPPERBROW": "Bram Copperbrow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BRAM_COPPERBROW_PEDIA": "The Chain of Little Mills[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Bram was sent to retrieve a broken golem that had kept a village's mill turning. He rebuilt its mechanism as a chain of small waterwheels that any farmer could service, then taught the village to cast replacements. Guild owners still send him bills for every grain of flour they think would have passed through that golem's hands.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DESSA_QUARTZBELL": "Dessa Quartzbell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DESSA_QUARTZBELL_PEDIA": "First of the Night Shift[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dessa automated a bell so workers below ground would hear the opening of the meeting that set their wages. When the masters moved the meeting to daylight, she moved the bell to the night market and invited everybody still awake. Her assembly votes beneath a quartz dome that used to be sealed after dusk.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NORI_BONDHAMMER": "Nori Bondhammer",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NORI_BONDHAMMER_PEDIA": "The Holder of the Keys[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nori sold his first golems cheaply after apprenticing under a master who could never get anyone to buy one. Every joint answered to a key only he could replace, and in time the public pumps all took the same locks. He is building a mint now so the people buying those keys will have somewhere to borrow their fee.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ULNA_SHALEPRINT": "Ulna Shaleprint",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ULNA_SHALEPRINT_PEDIA": "Caller of the Second Draft[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ulna hauled rejected molds back to the gallery where trainees were allowed to practice. She put a number on each one and paid a prize for improving the molds their inventors insisted were perfect. The trainees set up their own guild the day she stopped charging visitors to look at what they made.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KEDRAN_SHIELDPEG": "Kedran Shieldpeg",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KEDRAN_SHIELDPEG_PEDIA": "Warden of the Ground Floor[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Kedran discovered how far a broken protective seal could throw a cobblestone when one broke in his cellar. He mapped the streets that would be flattened if the guild towers failed, and their tenants began taking his inspections seriously. Even the tower masters now knock before stepping into a house that flies his mark.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PHILIA_COPPERROOF": "Philia Copperroof",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PHILIA_COPPERROOF_PEDIA": "The Canopy Surveyor[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Philia spent her first wages replacing a scaffold that humans could not reach and her fellow harpies were forbidden to land on. She kept the invoices and drew the first survey of all the roofs a city could not maintain without them. The workers now run the rooftop stations and keep a lane open even to people who cannot afford to fly.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RYTHON_TWELVEARCHES": "Rython Twelvearches",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RYTHON_TWELVEARCHES_PEDIA": "First Speaker of the Covered Market[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]A centaur's hooves once cracked the stairs to the market and Rython was charged for the damage although he was standing outside. He bought a sunken quarry and filled it with gentle ramps, high stalls and aisles wide enough for three bodies like his. He charges to cover the paving and lets even the people who sneered at it come in out of the rain.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISIL_COILBANK": "Isil Coilbank",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISIL_COILBANK_PEDIA": "The Holder of the Deeds[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Isil amassed deeds to the old riverbed while its lamia houses were still built on floats. When the river shifted inland, she charged the residents for the reclaimed foundations under their homes and took their boats as collateral. Her tenants are free to move, provided they leave their furnishings and a child who knows the ledger.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEVIN_MANYWINDOWS": "Tevin Manywindows",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEVIN_MANYWINDOWS_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Common Rooms[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tevin inherited a boarding house whose forebears had added a door for each new kind of lodger. He took the doors out of the price of rooms and set up the first tenants' festival with the proceeds from the old hinges. Visitors still take the number of doors for a sign of disorder until they see who carries whom out in a fire.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ASEN_CURTAINFALL": "Asen Curtainfall",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ASEN_CURTAINFALL_PEDIA": "Surveyor of the Last Street[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Asen grew up in a settlement that vanished each time the capital commissioned a new map. She sewed her street onto the edge of the maps and offered the rest of the bolt to other villages missing from the survey. The patchwork hangs in her court because it takes a succession of people to carry it anywhere else.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CALEN_BIRDBRANCH": "Calen Birdbranch",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CALEN_BIRDBRANCH_PEDIA": "The Messenger of the Lower Boughs[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Calen tended messenger birds that were trained to fly only to the manors in their breeding book. He let a clutch nest in a roadside willow and raised the fledglings to land where woodcutters offered a perch. The lord of the high branches still receives the news, a little later than the bark peelers.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MEVARA_RAINSUTURE": "Mevara Rainsuture",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MEVARA_RAINSUTURE_PEDIA": "Tender of the Root Bridges[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]When a flood took her bridge, Mevara followed its torn limbs downstream and transplanted the living ends. They took root on opposite banks within sight of an old ferry that had charged an armful of firewood to carry a child. Crossing dues in her new groves consist of watering a branch on the way past.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THALAN_AMBERVOW": "Thalan Ambervow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_THALAN_AMBERVOW_PEDIA": "The Warden of First Growth[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Thalan was a witness when the oldest pine of his province fell across a boundary ditch. In the resin he claimed to read a deed covering both banks, then the houses and fields adjoining them. He invites any dispossessed farmer to attend the hearings, provided they can offer testimony in a language the court accepts from trees.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FINNEL_WOODMETER": "Finnel Woodmeter",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FINNEL_WOODMETER_PEDIA": "The Tender of Coppices[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Finnel never felled a trunk he could return to in seven years and cut again. He taught the villages to count regrowth alongside their timber before the merchants could lay claim to it. His contracts infuriate the court because coppices are now worth enough that a worker can take a lord to court for stealing one.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LETHIEL_HOLLOWTONE": "Lethiel Hollowtone",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LETHIEL_HOLLOWTONE_PEDIA": "The Constant Root[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Lethiel suspected the humming that preceded a blight came from ordinary roots rather than a family spirit. She sank hollow tubes at each farm and invited the youngest worker to tell her the note before the family elders could interpret it. A ring of groves now reports to her when the note changes, sometimes in time to cut a branch and save a valley.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BERGA_IRONWEDGE": "Berga Ironwedge",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BERGA_IRONWEDGE_PEDIA": "First Hand of the Rescue Crew[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Berga's crew came to a collapsed adit with orders to save the ore chests before the men. She wedged the chests under a crack and hauled out enough witnesses to overturn the order in court. The holds that ignored the judgment still keep their pit bosses, but each deep crew carries a copy of her rescue charter.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORIK_CINDERHALL": "Orik Cinderhall",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORIK_CINDERHALL_PEDIA": "The Last Voice at the Smelter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Orik wanted to know why his apprentices froze upstairs while his slag boiled beneath their feet. He rerouted the waste heat through a row of affordable apartments before the hold's surveyors found out. The families bought the next furnace themselves and sent him to bargain for the mountain's water.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HULDRA_SEVENVAULT": "Huldra Sevenvault",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HULDRA_SEVENVAULT_PEDIA": "The Keeper of Quiet Interest[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Huldra bought a vault that had been locked since an earthquake and leased the idea of the gold within it to desperate holds. She offered to forgive any hold that opened the vault, so long as it signed over its rights to the gold if it failed. Now that seven tunnels have collapsed in the attempt, her creditors prefer to keep paying and let the door remain a matter of faith.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GARUM_VEINWALKER": "Garum Veinwalker",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GARUM_VEINWALKER_PEDIA": "Voice of the Surface Consuls[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Garum returned from shipping season with grain, new words and a wife who had never seen a vault. The keepers assigned them a cellar outside the gates, so he made it a warehouse and invited the coast hands to live nearby. The first surface consul was elected in that cellar with the shutters open.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_URSI_MOTHSILVER": "Ursi Mothsilver",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_URSI_MOTHSILVER_PEDIA": "Finder of Lost Silver[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ursi followed a tarnished thread into a shaft where all the assay marks had been chiseled away. She strung salvaged silver together by the sequence of tool marks and found a generation of makers the vault families never recorded. Neighboring holds send her strips of their own silver to see whose work she can name.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NARESH_LINTELMIST": "Naresh Lintelmist",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NARESH_LINTELMIST_PEDIA": "The Porter at Noon[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Naresh kept a toll station where travellers could shelter from the hour the shades were most visible. He repaired the shutters after every raid, leaving one unlatched for people who could no longer grasp the handle. Ordinary travellers now leave their names at the station beside the shades they still recognize.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAELIS_FROSTLINEN": "Maelis Frostlinen",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAELIS_FROSTLINEN_PEDIA": "The Weaver of the Missing Years[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Maelis began weaving dates into the strips she used to bind her winter clothes. Other shades brought her a loose end here and there, most of them as eager to forget it as to have it recorded. Her workshop keeps the resulting cloth folded so a visitor can ask for just the year she wants to see.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOREN_OF_THE_SPAN": "Soren of the Span",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOREN_OF_THE_SPAN_PEDIA": "The Toll on Vanished Things[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Soren built a bridge broad enough for the dead to cross without brushing the living. He began the toll with a coin and later agreed to accept an image the traveller could bear to lose. People cross more often now because he cannot collect the full fee from anyone who remembers what he first promised.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IRAM_STONEPAUSE": "Iram Stonepause",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IRAM_STONEPAUSE_PEDIA": "Mason of the Slow Stair[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Iram inherited a stair that was renewed every forty years by workers who did not live to see it finished. He carved a channel to take each course's weight before the next crew arrived, then invited the shades who had laid the lowest courses to check his work. The stair was finished in his lifetime, and the patches keep his apprentices employed in the next one.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VEDA_SECONDWINDOW": "Veda Secondwindow",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VEDA_SECONDWINDOW_PEDIA": "The Hush of the Dayroom[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Veda kept a room with the blinds open for her brother after he began to fade. He stopped recognizing the street, but his hand always found the window catch he had repaired as a boy. Her dayrooms now leave the things their visitors remember in reach and ask living guests to go home without carrying them away.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DARIO_REEFGLASS": "Dario Reefglass",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DARIO_REEFGLASS_PEDIA": "Pilot of the Shifting Bar[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dario learned to read silt by looking through the neck of a broken bottle a freebooter left behind. He warned three captains away from the bar the week the harbor lord's own ship went down. The pilots who inherit his bottle earn a berth each year by bringing at least one rival's ship safely home.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAKENA_SALTSTITCH": "Makena Saltstitch",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MAKENA_SALTSTITCH_PEDIA": "Admiral of the Dry Dock[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Makena scraped the governor's crest off a wreck and built the dock's doors out of its stern. The governor's creditors sued for the doors and found every crew in the harbor had bought a share in them. Warships still dock there, though their captains must wait while the smallest fishing boats come in first.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CARLON_MIRRORSAIL": "Carlon Mirrorsail",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CARLON_MIRRORSAIL_PEDIA": "Prince of the False Harbor[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Carlon survived a wreck on a coast where lights were supposed to show safe water. He spent his compensation on brighter lamps and bribed the surveyors to take his stretch of cliff off their charts. The colony that strips the stranded ships has enough timber for houses and too much rope for most of its workers to leave.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HESSA_SABLETIDE": "Hessa Sabletide",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HESSA_SABLETIDE_PEDIA": "The Uncrowned Diver[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Hessa cut loose a divers' bell the admiralty had declared lost while two men were still shut inside it. She entered the salvage award in their names and used her own share to buy communal lines and floats. The crews who train with her are trying to put the same bell to work on a shipwreck the admiralty has refused to admit is there.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JERON_FOGHORN": "Jeron Foghorn",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JERON_FOGHORN_PEDIA": "The Morning Between Storms[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Jeron kept watch for a storm his ship's priest had said would come only if somebody broke a vow. Every ship that followed his forecast instead survived, and the rest of the crews left him their instruments. He moors between hostile ports so that none of them can claim the weather belongs to its god alone.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TESSA_INKCLASP": "Tessa Inkclasp",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TESSA_INKCLASP_PEDIA": "Mistress of the Cheap Press[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tessa set her first type from lead she scavenged in a shrine the congregation had abandoned. She printed the survivors' account of its burning for less than the price of the priest's official version. The presses that learned from hers have turned the market's broadest street into a place where every lie has to compete for paper.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DOWEN_CAMPBRIDGE": "Dowen Campbridge",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DOWEN_CAMPBRIDGE_PEDIA": "Speaker for the Returning Companies[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dowen spent a decade dividing salvage between people who came back from the expedition and the families of those who did not. He made a rule about whose turn it was to talk and kept his camp open whenever a judge refused to hear a case. His court handles less patrol pay these days than quarrels over orchards, alleys and absent children.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IRENA_DREGS": "Irena Dregs",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IRENA_DREGS_PEDIA": "The Price of Plain Water[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Irena diluted the town's suspect well water with her own, expecting the council to reimburse her. When it refused, she bought the well rights and stopped telling her customers which water had been treated. Her settlements are prosperous enough to get every child a fresh cup, so long as its parents can keep up with the subscription.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GAVREN_ROOK": "Gavren Rook",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GAVREN_ROOK_PEDIA": "Warden of the Common Stacks[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Gavren inherited six chests of confiscated books and explicit instructions to burn them in public. He burned the chests and moved the contents to any household willing to lend them again. When a magistrate complained that he was running a shrine, he offered to put the complaint in circulation beside the rest of the holdings.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARA_BENTNAIL": "Mara Bentnail",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARA_BENTNAIL_PEDIA": "Captain of the Salvage Road[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Mara found a set of nail marks beside the spells that had made a collapsed tower impassable. She showed her crew how to recognize the safe route and marked every unclaimed chamber they freed. The city's owner sent a revenue guard and discovered someone had nailed his deed to the door of the one room she still refused to open.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PARNA_DAWNREIN": "Parna Dawnrein",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PARNA_DAWNREIN_PEDIA": "Speaker for the Unridden[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Parna kept count of the mares left behind when a chief made each household supply a second mount. She withheld the foals the next year and taught the other herders to quote a price for all the work between one campaign and another. A new company gets pasture from her only after one of its officers spends a season working with the mares.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEKAR_WINDSTITCH": "Bekar Windstitch",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEKAR_WINDSTITCH_PEDIA": "The Saddle That Fits[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Bekar rode with thirteen companies trying to learn why each one wore out the same seam. He designed a saddle whose parts any rider could exchange at his wagon, then made the wagons exchange parts with each other. Stocks of Bekar's bolts are now a better measure of a camp's wealth than the splendor of its tents.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESSA_REDSCRIP": "Nessa Redscrip",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESSA_REDSCRIP_PEDIA": "Mistress of the Standing Retainer[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nessa offered to pay the winter wages of a company whose employer had fled. She paid in leather scrip redeemable at her own corrals and gave the officers twice as much when they seized the nearest crossing. In spring they found the retainer had no end date and the ford no road outside her accounting.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORALUN_HAILWARD": "Oralun Hailward",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORALUN_HAILWARD_PEDIA": "The Truce at Foaling Hill[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Oralun lost the mare that had carried him through six engagements when two camps fought over the only sheltered ground. He dug a trough there and swore to turn his bow on the first rider who crossed it armed. The elders still complain about that oath while sending their own mares to graze within its terms.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEDI_HORSETHISTLE": "Sedi Horsethistle",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SEDI_HORSETHISTLE_PEDIA": "Master of the Mare's Mile[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sedi's first mare outran her captain's horse on road work and was sold away before she could enter a race. She left the company with the next horse and timed an unsanctioned mile along every ford their riders used. Rich camps send their favorites to win her ribbons and sometimes have to buy the winner a coat to wear at the feast.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FARIEN_COPPERSPARK": "Farien Copperspark",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_FARIEN_COPPERSPARK_PEDIA": "The First Bench at Dusk[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Farien discovered that the apprentices who swept her laboratory could reproduce the simplest lights without seeing the instructions. She offered to teach them in a public yard and let them decide when a lesson was over. The academies no longer know whom to fine, since on some evenings an entire neighborhood comes out to make its lamps burn.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TALLIS_BLUEPUMICE": "Tallis Bluepumice",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TALLIS_BLUEPUMICE_PEDIA": "The Inspector of Wells[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Tallis stopped taking the academy's word about waste after his tools floated out of a public cistern. He trained a team to test the water on pumice before it entered the drains and to close any gate that made the stone shine. The guild's inspections are unpopular enough that several wizards quietly pay him to repeat them at their own wells.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISHARA_MORROWQUILL": "Ishara Morrowquill",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ISHARA_MORROWQUILL_PEDIA": "The Mistress of Closed Instruction[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ishara learned to bind little household spirits to unfinished examinations and sold their help to students in disgrace. She agreed to wait for payment until the students had laboratories of their own. Her former pupils send whatever she requests because the spirit that took an examination can always come back to dispute its mark.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CORIN_SLATEGATE": "Corin Slategate",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_CORIN_SLATEGATE_PEDIA": "The Marshal of Locked Doors[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Corin led the first rescue crew into a laboratory without asking each occupant's academy rank. The order to arrest him came from the surviving masters once they found he had taken the injured servants out first. He spent his sentence drawing a plan of all the locked doors a municipal watch would need to open at the next fire.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MERI_THUNDERCHALK": "Meri Thunderchalk",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MERI_THUNDERCHALK_PEDIA": "The Gallery of Failed Wonders[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Meri built a room to exhibit dangerous artifacts that would no longer obey their inventors. She invited each inventor to demonstrate the safest way an ordinary visitor could carry the artifact back out. The gallery is crowded enough that inventors threaten to show her their rivals' successes if she puts their disasters too near the door.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RUNA_BOLDHIDE": "Runa Boldhide",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RUNA_BOLDHIDE_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Unfrozen Camp[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Runa remembered which valley would thaw early because she had buried six sled runners there one winter. She spread the first hides over their graves while her crew dug a shelter deep enough to outlast the snow. The next war band that wanted to requisition her winter stores found every family expecting a vote.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MORGUN_ICEHEEL": "Morgun Iceheel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MORGUN_ICEHEEL_PEDIA": "Guide of the Blue Notch[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Morgun turned a scouting party back at a cut in the glacier where he could hear meltwater under solid ice. Half the party laughed and went on while he spent the night making crampons for the rest. He still takes commissions from captains who doubt his stories, so long as they understand which of them he gets to turn back.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEKKA_TALLFANG": "Bekka Tallfang",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BEKKA_TALLFANG_PEDIA": "Voice of the Empty Den[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Bekka claimed she could tell which family a wolf had eaten by the shape of its teeth. When one den lay empty after a raid she took its cub and visited an enemy family to collect the milk she said it was owed. The families that refuse to feed it have begun waking to find a tooth beside their oldest child's bed.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JEREK_BARKSHIELD": "Jerek Barkshield",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JEREK_BARKSHIELD_PEDIA": "Friend of the Winter Strangers[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Jerek carried fallen rivals over the ice rather than let their families follow the spring water to look for them. The families taught his camp the ways they cured bark, wove willow and stopped a frostbitten toe going black. His successor has to know a trade somebody else brought across the river, which makes it hard for the old chiefs to agree on one.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UNNA_ASHTRAIL": "Unna Ashtrail",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UNNA_ASHTRAIL_PEDIA": "The Last Word at the Fire[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Unna kept a stick for every night two kin groups managed to share a shelter without drawing blades. She made their loudest fighters break one before the whole camp whenever they wanted to resume an argument. The bundle grew so heavy she could send an apprentice to collect their fines and still have enough sticks for the night.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PELLA_CRACKMIRROR": "Pella Crackmirror",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_PELLA_CRACKMIRROR_PEDIA": "Marshal of the Repaired Faces[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Pella repaired a child's mask and realized the child had never seen the inside of it. She made a hook for each mask that came to the shop and set a rule that no visitor could be called by a role until reaching for one. The hooks fill quickest after the court announces an especially heartwarming show.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARNO_REDBUTTON": "Marno Redbutton",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARNO_REDBUTTON_PEDIA": "Proprietor of the Other Ending[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Marno brought down the curtain on a tragedy a full scene early and heard the audience finish it three different ways. He carried that trick to a court whose members had paid him to make an enemy look foolish. They have not hired him since, but their neighbors keep asking how many other endings they could afford.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JESSA_OF_THE_RAKED_SEATS": "Jessa of the Raked Seats",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_JESSA_OF_THE_RAKED_SEATS_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Laughing Debt[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Jessa ran the theater's debt book from the cheap seats because the back row had the best view of who walked out. She put a small fee on departures and waived it for anyone who could find a stranger to take a seat. Her new hall is always full, a fact the court displays with considerable pride.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NIVARO_SPRINGHEEL": "Nivaro Springheel",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NIVARO_SPRINGHEEL_PEDIA": "The Parade Below Ground[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nivaro put a cart of failed parade floats below the city to keep them out of a rainstorm. When a new arrest order came down, he rolled them away with the wanted performers hidden inside. He still plans the underground procession every year, and its most important spectators wait by the exits with food.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SILL_NEEDLEMOON": "Sill Needlemoon",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SILL_NEEDLEMOON_PEDIA": "The Volunteer at Every Rehearsal[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sill learned to put her fear of heights in a glass bead before walking the rope. She made spare beads for the other performers and charged spectators who wanted to feel what bravery had cost them. The ones she has not sold accumulate in the wings, where a wrong step makes an audience of a single person.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GROMKA_POTLID": "Gromka Potlid",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_GROMKA_POTLID_PEDIA": "The Hearth That Feeds Strangers[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Gromka cooked three pots at an exhausted camp and discovered the smallest pot fed the most people if everyone ate in turns. She took it along the line of camps and began asking each one what it could bring to the next meal. Their storehouses are tall enough now that the chiefs must come down from their war seats to ask for a share.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BORRUK_OREJAW": "Borruk Orejaw",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_BORRUK_OREJAW_PEDIA": "Warden of the Spear Ferry[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Borruk was told to tear down a ferry before a rival band's march and wait on the far bank for orders. He stretched a spare chain across the river instead and carried both sides a wagon at a time, after collecting a broken spear as the fare. He has enough iron for a new ferry now and more armed friends on either bank than the chief who gave the order.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UZHKA_SOURBONE": "Uzhka Sourbone",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_UZHKA_SOURBONE_PEDIA": "Collector of the Foster Tithe[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Uzhka raised the child of a visiting chief in good faith until the chief refused to send her son home. She counted the weeks she had fed his child and demanded a second from every family that spoke for him. The terms are carved on notched bones so that even a child can read how long she intends to keep them.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RASK_GLOWCAP": "Rask Glowcap",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RASK_GLOWCAP_PEDIA": "The Keeper of Low Roads[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Rask grew a shining fungus in the boot he had been issued because its mate had never arrived. His family planted spores along a route the orc patrols could not crawl through, and the route proved faster than the march above. When he began charging to carry messages, the captains wanted to know where he got the advantage and the boot.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MOGRA_TEMPREED": "Mogra Tempreed",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MOGRA_TEMPREED_PEDIA": "Speaker of the Reed Shields[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Mogra brought a reed shield to a challenge after no smith would sell iron to her family. She let the first opponent put three holes in it before showing how the reeds closed around a blade. Every marsh camp knows how to weave her shields now, and their young fighters have stopped asking for someone else's colors.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MIREL_WIDOWFERN": "Mirel Widowfern",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MIREL_WIDOWFERN_PEDIA": "The Warden of Surrender[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Mirel carried a ransom to a council that had already voted to kill her brother. She left the payment on the floor and brought home its youngest guard, who had opened the cellar when nobody else would. Her safe houses fly the guard's badge in reverse and offer the same deal to anybody willing to turn a key.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOVAR_WHISPERLEAF": "Sovar Whisperleaf",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SOVAR_WHISPERLEAF_PEDIA": "The Exchange of Safe Rumors[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sovar was paid for a rumor so good his customer tried to have him killed to stop it being sold twice. He moved into an abandoned theater and fixed a fee for each warning, deducted if the customer could prove it false. The best seats are reserved for the people who correct him in public, which keeps his oldest customers away.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ITHRA_BLACKVINE": "Ithra Blackvine",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ITHRA_BLACKVINE_PEDIA": "Keeper of the Still-Verdict[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ithra bred a vine that remembered the sound of its keeper's last command. She grew it about a deserted judgment seat and announced that it could hear evidence the living had learned to forget. Speakers now agree with the vine while it is still rustling, in the hope of keeping what they meant to say out of reach.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESSAR_FOXGLOVE": "Nessar Foxglove",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NESSAR_FOXGLOVE_PEDIA": "Gardener of the Truce Path[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Nessar planted the winter gardens of houses that were fighting about whose road could reach the wells. He put his newest cuttings where nobody expected to walk and pruned them until two kitchens found themselves joined by a path. The servants use it in the dark, and their employers have learned to buy herbs from the same old man.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VALIRA_THINVEIL": "Valira Thinveil",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VALIRA_THINVEIL_PEDIA": "The Maker of Second Faces[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Valira dressed a runner in an expensive disguise that came apart at the first dawn gate. She began again with cheap wool, the accent of her sister's village, and a handful of genuine neighbors willing to vouch. The people she helps remember the invented childhoods less clearly than the real neighbor who arrived first to defend them.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VESNA_QUAYWARD": "Vesna Quayward",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_VESNA_QUAYWARD_PEDIA": "The Mortal Factor[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Vesna never owned land, which helped the customs officers overlook her first dozen cargoes of people. She paid a new wharf guard every month until the guard and the dockhands asked her to buy their entire company. The company publishes its tariff in the markets and charges less for a person whose master is likely to come looking.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MALREC_RUBYHAND": "Malrec Rubyhand",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MALREC_RUBYHAND_PEDIA": "The Lord of the Off Hours[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Malrec inherited a dye works whose masters were still signing orders long after he went below to rest. He began buying their experiments on the condition that the other lords could see their account books. Now the weavers elect their own night foreman, and the worst part of challenging him is finding a color he has not already bought.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LYSANA_BLOODGILT": "Lysana Bloodgilt",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_LYSANA_BLOODGILT_PEDIA": "The Inheritor of Feasts[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Lysana received only the silverware when her aunt's estates changed hands. She bought back the right to each of the meals once served on it and spent a decade demonstrating that the meals were worth more than the land. The silver now goes round her table at every auction while stewards outside count the villagers each bid will cost.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MILOS_GRAINSTY": "Milos Grainsty",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MILOS_GRAINSTY_PEDIA": "The Keeper of the Cellar Door[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Milos discovered a supply cellar the estate had walled shut during an outbreak of hunger. He reopened it and set one household to watch its door while he asked the others to leave what they could inside. The lord's reeve says the deficit is all due to rats, and nobody in the family has been unkind enough to contradict him.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DIANTHE_NIGHTJURY": "Dianthe Nightjury",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_DIANTHE_NIGHTJURY_PEDIA": "The Judge of the Open Shutter[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Dianthe could never prove whether a fellow elder had glamoured her bailiff or merely bribed him. She made a courtroom where the bailiff need only open a shutter to halt proceedings and put his account on the record. The bailiff sits a little more comfortably than was once customary, which may have as much to do with his honesty as the shutters.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEMRAN_CLOSETOWER": "Temran Closetower",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_TEMRAN_CLOSETOWER_PEDIA": "The Joiner of False Rooms[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Temran's first door opened into a room his workshop had never built. He sent a model through before he sent a worker and found the model's little furniture in the workshop two days before he finished it. His apprentices check their models for inhabitants before accepting the next gate commission.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IVAYA_FADEBELL": "Ivaya Fadebell",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_IVAYA_FADEBELL_PEDIA": "The Listener at the Safeline[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Ivaya tied a bell to a gate beam because at first any change in its note lasted long enough to let her fetch a master. By the time the note broke there was only time to knock on the neighboring doors. The new settlements around her watch towers built their meeting rooms far enough away that a bell can still be heard before it is needed.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORYS_REDMARGIN": "Orys Redmargin",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ORYS_REDMARGIN_PEDIA": "The Patron of Final Revisions[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Orys found he could bring a completed manuscript backward through a gate whose owner had never finished it. He invited writers to read what their work would cost them and submit a revision to the harm, provided they offered the gate a replacement. The margins in his notebook have grown crowded with other people's villages.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NERISS_CERULEAN_WAKE": "Neriss Cerulean Wake",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_NERISS_CERULEAN_WAKE_PEDIA": "The Ferryman of Close Shores[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Neriss charged twice for a first step into the beyond and nothing for the second. He found that his clients were willing to carry anyone they met back through, provided the fares went to the keeper of a permanent landing. The keepers have set their ceilings low so the guests who come out like the sound of a room the same height as the one they left.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HESIA_ASHCOMBER": "Hesia Ashcomber",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HESIA_ASHCOMBER_PEDIA": "Custodian of the Blue Orchard[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Hesia returned to her father's orchard and found it yielding ash in the size and shape of apples. She broke one open, recovered a seed and planted all the ones that sprouted blue in a new row. The fruit keeps a hungry village alive, although every grower takes a turn deciding whether the next batch can be trusted.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KAARAS_HINGESMOKE": "Kaaras Hingesmoke",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_KAARAS_HINGESMOKE_PEDIA": "The Keeper of the Unlatched Pit[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Kaaras's first guard shift began the day the terms of a dozen prisoners expired. He found no release order and spent his next century opening the lower gate one inmate at a time. The old masters finally noticed his absences when their favorite victims started selling him supplies on the other side.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SIVRIEL_CINNABAR": "Sivriel Cinnabar",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SIVRIEL_CINNABAR_PEDIA": "The Mistress of Ash Assays[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sivriel salvaged the ashes of failed infernal contracts and found that certain signatures gave off enough heat to temper iron. She tested which materials held that heat and bought the furnaces that could burn them. Now her buyers are required to bargain at least once a season whether they need anything or not.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MORDAX_SILVERCORD": "Mordax Silvercord",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MORDAX_SILVERCORD_PEDIA": "The Chancellor of Lesser Malice[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Mordax bought a minor siege engine and sent his agents to describe it to every town he could not reach. He collected the first tribute from a city that had never seen it and bought two smaller engines from the proceeds. He encourages his converts to underpay because collecting arrears pays his companies better than keeping a promise.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AETHI_VAULTMOTH": "Aethi Vaultmoth",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_AETHI_VAULTMOTH_PEDIA": "The Host of Unwanted Revenants[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Aethi had been ordered to frighten a soul that had already forgotten whom it had lost. Instead she told it to describe the last room it had slept in, which it could scarcely do without recalling a name. The chambers she made from such descriptions fill in turn with people who remember enough to ask for a different punishment.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ZURN_EMBERSEAL": "Zurn Emberseal",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ZURN_EMBERSEAL_PEDIA": "The Right of First Smoke[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Zurn once let a burning manor cool because three rival devils were still debating its claims. The departing souls fled past them in the smoke while he was forbidden to lift a finger. He took the first flare from the next fire into a bronze box, hired a force to carry it and told the rest of the claimants to come take it from him.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RHUNA_SNOWCELLAR": "Rhuna Snowcellar",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_RHUNA_SNOWCELLAR_PEDIA": "The Keeper of Tomorrow's Bread[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Rhuna stayed after her village began sending its young people south to find a second harvest. She opened an old ice cellar and packed the crop in shallow baskets that even a single household could lift. The cellar kept more people alive than she expected, and they insist on making decisions in its coldest room so nobody can forget what the bread costs.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARDEN_SLEETFOIL": "Marden Sleetfoil",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_MARDEN_SLEETFOIL_PEDIA": "Cartographer of the Open Drift[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Marden painted his first map on a panel the wind took into the next valley. By the time he recovered it the best route had drifted over and the first party had only survived by drawing its own. He prints an empty margin around every pass so that the people who find a better way can leave the next light burning.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ERVA_WHITECROWN": "Erva Whitecrown",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_ERVA_WHITECROWN_PEDIA": "The Cold of the Claims Court[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Erva counted the households on one slope by the smoke above their roofs and found more fires than taxpayers. She devised a charcoal allowance so that every chimney needing fuel had to file a return. Her deputies can close a case by closing a flue, which makes even the accurate returns quite expensive.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SIEDA_ICEQUILL": "Sieda Icequill",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_SIEDA_ICEQUILL_PEDIA": "The Keeper of the Thaw Tongue[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Sieda heard her grandmother give a different name to the same clearing each year as the snow crept down. She recorded each name beside its year and sang the sequence as spring travellers passed her door. Several villages that had been missing from the rolls for forty winters sent her word that they remembered the same places.",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HALEK_RIMEHOOK": "Halek Rimehook",
    "TXT_KEY_LEADER_HALEK_RIMEHOOK_PEDIA": "The Warden of Returning Fires[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]Halek found a town's tools lined up at the edge of a stretch of ice too thin to cross with them. He kept the ironware bright for three winters expecting their makers to return. After others came to claim it, he promised to give each tool to the first person who could stand at its old hearth and explain what it had been made to fix."
  }
}
